        "question": "What is the difference between a humanoid robot joint housing and a robot actuator housing?",
        "answer": "An actuator housing integrates the motor and gearbox, so its critical feature is the coaxiality chain among stator and bearing seats, usually held within 0.005&ndash;0.008mm. A joint housing is the external structural body: it carries bending and torsional loads and relies on +/-0.002mm bore diameter, +/-0.001mm roundness, and +/-0.003mm concentricity. Both use single-setup machining, but actuator housings prioritise motor-axis alignment while joint housings prioritise thin-wall free-state bore precision."
